CLAUDE.md
RULES.md
task.md
PLAN.md
PLAN-*.md
.claude/

# A local set of captures for the offline proofs to run against. These are NOT
# this project's to publish: a capture is a recording made by whoever trained
# it, under their own terms, and the code's MIT grant does not reach it. Neither
# product ships any and neither resolves a bank by a tracked path — the four
# banks are whatever the user loaded — so nothing here depends on this directory
# existing. The proofs find it through --captures or $RATIONS_TEST_CAPTURES.
#
# Keep it that way: a capture committed here is a capture republished to
# everyone who clones, and deleting it later does not take it back out of the
# history.
captures/

# The ASIO SDK, which must NEVER be committed here, in whole or in part.
#
# WHY THIS RULE OUTLIVED ITS ORIGINAL REASON. It began as a licence condition: under the
# proprietary arm of the SDK's dual licence, Steinberg's agreement forbids the licensee to "sell,
# license, give-away and/or distribute the SDK or parts of it for the use as software developer kit
# in any way, for example, integrated in any framework application, on any medium, including the
# Internet", so a committed copy was a breach that a later deletion did not undo.
#
# This project now takes the SDK's GPLv3 arm instead, and under GPLv3 redistributing its source is
# not merely permitted but REQUIRED of anyone shipping the binary -- as Corresponding Source. The
# prohibition is gone. The rule stays, for two reasons that are still good:
#
#   1. The tree stays free of a dependency it does not own. The SDK reaches the compiler from the
#      untracked sysroot, pinned by SHA-256, and reaches users through the Corresponding Source
#      tarball that scripts/corresponding-source.sh assembles at release time. Both are build
#      output. Neither is a checked-in copy that quietly drifts from what upstream ships.
#   2. It keeps the proprietary arm available. Committing the SDK is the one act that would close
#      that door permanently, and a licence election is worth being able to reverse.
#
# (The reference implementations still get this wrong: RtAudio vendors an ASIO snapshot
# in-repository and iPlug2 inherits it. It is not a precedent to follow.)
#
# The SDK reaches the Windows build through scripts/build-win-deps.sh -- one script, shared by both
# products since stage 7 -- and the untracked dependency sysroot, and through nothing else. These
# names are guarded so a stray copy cannot be staged by accident; the phase gate sweeps for the same
# names, because the ignore rule stops a copy arriving and the sweep catches one that arrived
# another way.
#
# AT THE ROOT AND UNANCHORED, deliberately. Only one product builds for ASIO today, but a stray
# copy can land anywhere in a tree this size, and a rule that only guarded products/rack/ would
# not have been watching the place the accident happened.
#
# Our own backend is asiobackend.{h,cpp}, which none of these patterns matches.
asio.h
asio.cpp
asiosys.h
iasiodrv.h
asiodrvr.h
asiodrvr.cpp
asiodrivers.h
asiodrivers.cpp
asiolist.h
asiolist.cpp
ASIOConvertSamples.h
ASIOConvertSamples.cpp

